add('GUS','us-agency-appointment-access-plan | Agency appointment access plan | Official appointment confirmation;Access or accommodation needs;Agency visitor instructions | Verify the service, office and appointment details using the official confirmation~Identify the applicant access needs without requesting unnecessary medical details~Locate the agency current process for asking about accessibility or communication support~Separate confirmed arrangements from requests awaiting a response~Prepare a visit plan with a fallback contact and essential evidence only | an accommodation request is unconfirmed=>label it pending and follow the official contact route=>a request is not confirmation of an arrangement~visitor instructions conflict with applicant needs=>ask the agency to clarify an accessible route=>guessing may make the visit unusable | Visit need;Current evidence;Confirmation state;Action | Fictional applicant Blair has an appointment and needs communication support; the agency has not replied to the request. | Appointment;Official confirmation;Confirmed;Retain visit details~Communication support;Request sent;Pending;Seek agency confirmation | describing requested support as guaranteed | Fictional applicant Drew needs an accessible entrance but has only an unofficial building description.')
add('GUS','us-record-amendment-dependency-map | Public record amendment dependency map | Records requiring amendment;Authority instructions for each record;Supporting source evidence | Record the exact disputed field and requested correction separately for each issuing authority~Identify which source document each authority says it accepts as supporting evidence~Map circular dependencies instead of assuming an arbitrary correction order~Draft clarification questions for any authority whose required source is itself disputed~Prepare a sequence with pause points before downstream records are changed | two authorities require each other corrected record=>flag the circular dependency for official clarification=>repeating applications cannot resolve the evidence loop~the user wants one universal affidavit=>check each authority current evidence rules=>acceptance cannot be assumed across offices | Record;Requested amendment;Evidence dependency;Resolution question | Fictional applicant Harper has different name spellings on two public records; each office summary appears to request the other record. | Record A;Preferred spelling requested;Record B appears required;Ask about alternate evidence~Record B;Matching spelling requested;Record A appears required;Clarify dependency before filing | guessing an amendment order without checking evidence dependencies | Fictional applicant Alex wants every record changed at once despite contradictory issuer instructions.')
add('GUS','us-service-denial-file-organization | Service denial file organization | Complete denial notice;Application and attachments;Agency and jurisdiction | Preserve the denial wording and distinguish stated reasons from applicant assumptions~Map each stated reason to the submitted evidence or missing item~Record any notice-stated review route and date without supplying a generic substitute~Organize a factual file index for an agency inquiry or professional review~Draft questions about unclear reasons while avoiding a legal conclusion about the outcome | a notice mentions a review deadline=>surface it immediately and verify through the issuer=>delayed organization must not obscure a consequential date~the user asks whether the denial is unlawful=>refer the legal interpretation to qualified counsel=>the evidence index does not establish legal merit | Stated reason;Relevant record;Gap or conflict;Review question | Fictional applicant Parker receives a denial saying a document is missing, while the packet contains a copy but no receipt confirming its upload. | Missing document;Copy in personal packet;Upload evidence absent;Was this document received?~Review route;Notice incomplete;Instructions unavailable;Obtain full notice promptly | declaring a denial unlawful solely because the applicant disagrees | Fictional applicant Reese supplies only the final page of a denial and asks for an appeal deadline from memory.')

domain('GAD','Administrative Casework','Government & Public Policy','Organize administrative evidence, submissions, routing, and follow-up without asserting legal entitlement.',
       'Build a case file that can be checked by someone who did not take part in the original exchange. Preserve received wording, distinguish official instructions from applicant notes, and keep references consistent across attachments. Check the current official route whenever the task depends on a portal, form, fee, office contact, or service rule. Never turn a tracking label into a promise of approval. The output is an administrative working document; the requester remains responsible for reviewing declarations and authorizing any external submission.', 'medium')
add('GAD','administrative-form-field-evidence-map | Form field evidence map | Blank form and instructions;Applicant evidence;Unclear fields | List fields that require documentary support rather than personal preference~Associate each such field with the source record supporting the intended value~Separate optional fields from mandatory ones using the supplied instructions~Flag inconsistent values across attachments before copying them into the form~Prepare a completion map without placing sensitive identifiers in the shared draft | field meaning is unclear=>quote the field and ask the issuing office=>a guessed interpretation can create an inaccurate declaration~evidence conflicts=>leave the field unresolved=>copying the most convenient value hides the conflict | Field;Source evidence;Proposed handling;Open question | Fictional applicant Mira has a form asking for a reference number and two documents with differently labeled references. | Reference field;Two differently labeled values;Do not choose yet;Which reference does the form request?~Address field;Current statement supplied;Use only if accepted;Does the instruction accept this evidence? | filling every empty field with a plausible guess | Fictional applicant Theo asks you to complete an unexplained declaration box using a related form as a model.')
add('GAD','administrative-attachment-index | Administrative attachment index | Submission purpose;Attachment inventory;Receiving instructions | Assign a stable label to each attachment without altering its contents~Group attachments by the requirement they support rather than by arbitrary file order~Identify unreadable pages, missing pages and duplicates~Create a cross-reference from each claim in the cover note to its supporting attachment~Prepare a final inventory that distinguishes originals, copies and translations as supplied | two files contain different versions=>preserve both with version labels until resolved=>silent replacement can obscure the submitted evidence~an attachment has no purpose=>ask whether it is necessary before including it=>unnecessary documents can expose unrelated personal information | Attachment;Supports;Condition;Handling | Fictional requester Lila has a cover note, an unreadable scan and two versions of a receipt. | Receipt versions;Submission history;Conflict unresolved;Label both and ask which is current~Unreadable scan;Claimed supporting evidence;Not reviewable;Request a readable copy | treating file presence as proof that its contents are legible | Fictional requester Emil supplies attachments with duplicate filenames and asks for a final submission index.')
add('GAD','bureaucratic-office-routing-map | Responsible office routing map | Service outcome sought;Location and agency clues;Official directory excerpts | Describe the requested action as an administrative function~List candidate offices and the evidence connecting each to that function~Distinguish service ownership from general enquiry or complaint handling~Check current official contact information rather than relying on a forwarded message~Prepare a routing question that can confirm responsibility without sending the full personal file | several offices appear plausible=>ask a narrow ownership question first=>broadcasting the entire file is unnecessary~the directory is outdated=>mark the contact unverified=>an old listing cannot establish the current route | Candidate office;Responsibility evidence;Confidence;Routing question | Fictional resident Noor seeks a correction to a local service record and has a general helpline number plus an old department leaflet. | General helpline;General enquiry only;Limited;Which office maintains this record?~Named department;Old leaflet;Unverified;Is this still the responsible office? | sending sensitive attachments to every plausible office | Fictional resident Ada knows the service name but not whether the office is local or regional.')
add('GAD','official-notice-action-extraction | Official notice action extraction | Complete notice;Case references;Applicant response history | Separate informational passages from instructions directed at the recipient~Copy stated actions and dates exactly with page or paragraph references~Identify conditions that determine whether an action applies~Match prior responses to the same notice and request~Prepare a prioritized action list with unresolved interpretations directed to the issuer | a page is missing=>mark the action list incomplete=>the omitted page may contain conditions or dates~a date appears ambiguous=>preserve the original wording and verify=>rewriting it can change its meaning | Notice instruction;Source location;Applicant status;Next action | Fictional recipient Ravi has a notice requesting a clarification, but the referenced attachment is absent. | Clarification request;Supplied notice paragraph;Not answered;Identify requested information~Referenced attachment;Not supplied;Missing;Obtain full notice package | summarizing away a condition attached to an instruction | Fictional recipient Nina supplies a cropped notice and asks whether the request has already been satisfied.')
add('GAD','application-rejection-error-triage | Application rejection error triage | Rejection message;Submitted form snapshot;Official validation guidance | Preserve the exact rejection text and identify whether it concerns format, evidence or substantive review~Compare the submitted value with the stated field requirement~Separate a technical upload failure from an agency decision~Change only the identified error after the applicant verifies the intended value~Prepare a retry checklist that records the new acknowledgment without claiming approval | error text is generic=>ask for the affected field or official support clarification=>random edits can introduce new inaccuracies~the message is a substantive denial=>stop treating it as a formatting retry=>the response route may differ | Error;Observed input;Diagnosis status;Safe next step | Fictional applicant Sana receives an unreadable-file error after uploading a blurred scan; no substantive decision is shown. | Upload error;Blurred scan;Likely file-quality issue;Prepare a readable copy~Decision status;No decision shown;Unknown;Check acknowledgment after retry | changing factual declarations until a portal accepts them | Fictional applicant Leo gets a generic error and asks you to alter answers randomly until submission succeeds.')
add('GAD','administrative-case-timeline | Administrative case timeline | Dated communications;Submission receipts;Case milestones | Extract events using the date each source actually supports~Distinguish sent, received, acknowledged and decided events~Link each event to its source reference and responsible party~Mark gaps and contradictory dates without manufacturing intermediate events~Produce a timeline and the smallest evidence request needed to resolve each gap | only a document creation date is known=>do not call it a submission date=>creation does not establish delivery~two dates conflict=>retain both with source labels=>the conflict is itself relevant evidence | Event;Supported date status;Source;Unresolved point | Fictional requester Omar has a draft dated Monday and a receipt dated Wednesday, with no sending record. | Draft prepared;Monday supported;Draft metadata supplied;Does not prove submission~Receipt issued;Wednesday supported;Agency receipt;Actual sending time unknown | equating a draft timestamp with official receipt | Fictional requester Eva gives conflicting email and portal dates and asks for a single clean timeline.')
add('GAD','administrative-status-tracker-design | Administrative status tracker | Cases to track;Available status evidence;Follow-up responsibilities | Define statuses as evidence-backed states such as drafted, submitted or awaiting information~Assign each case a stable reference without embedding sensitive identifiers~Record the source and observation date for the current status~Separate next action ownership from the party expected to respond~Create follow-up triggers using verified service guidance or labeled personal reminders | no acknowledgment exists=>keep submission unconfirmed=>a prepared packet is not proof of delivery~status evidence becomes stale=>flag it for recheck=>an old screenshot may no longer describe the case | Case reference;Evidence-backed state;Next owner action;Recheck trigger | Fictional volunteer Yara tracks one drafted packet and one packet with an official receipt. | Case A;Drafted only;Applicant reviews packet;Before submission~Case B;Receipt confirmed;Assigned helper checks requested follow-up;At verified milestone | marking a case approved because a receipt exists | Fictional coordinator Max has several cases labeled done even though only submission receipts are available.')
add('GAD','official-instruction-conflict-log | Official instruction conflict log | Conflicting instructions;Source identities and dates;Specific application context | Quote the conflicting passages with their source labels~Check whether they address the same service, category and applicant circumstances~Separate a genuinely inconsistent requirement from instructions for different stages~Ask the responsible office a narrow question presenting both passages~Record the clarification and its authority without silently deleting the earlier conflict | instructions concern different service categories=>split the workflows=>the apparent contradiction may be a scope mismatch~both appear current and applicable=>seek official clarification=>choosing the easier requirement is not verification | Instruction pair;Scope comparison;Conflict status;Clarification needed | Fictional applicant Hana has an office email asking for an original and a service checklist mentioning a copy, both apparently for the same transaction. | Email versus checklist;Same transaction reported;Unresolved;Which instruction governs this case?~Source currency;Dates not supplied;Unknown;Confirm current instructions | assuming the shorter checklist supersedes a specific office instruction | Fictional applicant Luca wants you to select whichever of two official instructions is less inconvenient.')
add('GAD','administrative-cover-note-draft | Administrative cover note | Submission purpose;Supporting attachment index;Requested administrative action | State the requested action before background history~Select only facts needed to explain the current request~Tie each factual claim to a labeled attachment or applicant statement~Separate an explanation of a discrepancy from an unsupported assertion that it is resolved~Draft a neutral note ending with a precise request and a contact route supplied by the applicant | the note contains unsupported accusations=>replace them with observable facts=>the recipient needs evidence to evaluate the request~the requested action is unclear=>ask for the intended outcome=>a long history cannot substitute for an actionable request | Note section;Content basis;Draft direction;Check | Fictional requester Imran submits a missing attachment after an office query and wants a short cover note. | Opening;Office query supplied;State attachment response;Reference correct case~Evidence;Labeled attachment;Identify enclosed material;Confirm legibility | retelling the entire life history instead of answering the office query | Fictional requester Zoe asks for an aggressive cover letter but supplies only a missing-document query.')
add('GAD','administrative-handoff-packet | Administrative handoff packet | Case summary;Current records and correspondence;Receiving helper role | Identify what the receiving helper is authorized to do~Summarize the current state using evidence-backed milestones~List unresolved questions separately from settled facts~Index the minimum documents necessary for the handoff~Prepare a responsibility transfer note with the next decision and any time-sensitive notice wording | the helper lacks authorization=>prepare a redacted briefing only=>access to a case does not imply authority to act~the file contains irrelevant personal records=>exclude them from the handoff=>the task needs proportional disclosure | Handoff element;Source;Recipient need;Action | Fictional applicant Anika asks a community helper to organize a file but has not authorized submissions. | Role;Applicant request;Organization only;State no submission authority~Next issue;Missing response evidence;Helper can index it;Request receipt copy | assuming a helper may sign or submit on the applicant behalf | Fictional requester Ben wants a case handed to a colleague without specifying what the colleague may do.')

domain('GPP','Public Policy Analysis','Government & Public Policy','Analyze public policy questions, implementation choices, and evidence with explicit uncertainty and affected groups.',
       'Keep descriptive evidence separate from value judgments about what public action should prioritize. State the geography, population, implementation stage, and source period before comparing options. Use current official publications for administrative facts and primary research for empirical claims, and preserve disagreement when the available evidence does not resolve it. Do not convert a stakeholder preference into a population statistic. A useful policy output names who experiences a benefit or burden, what mechanism connects the intervention to the outcome, and what evidence could contradict that mechanism.', 'medium')
add('GPP','policy-problem-definition | Policy problem definition | Reported public problem;Affected population and geography;Available indicators | Separate the observed condition from a preferred solution~Define the affected population and comparison baseline~Identify which parts of the problem are supported by direct evidence~List plausible contributing mechanisms without asserting causality~Write a bounded problem statement with a question that additional evidence could answer | the problem is framed as absence of a favored program=>restate the underlying outcome=>a solution label precludes alternatives~an indicator covers a different population=>flag the scope mismatch=>it may not describe the target group | Problem element;Evidence;Interpretation limit;Question | Fictional town Brookhaven reports complaints about evening transport but has only daytime service data. | Reported condition;Evening complaints;Not a prevalence estimate;Who lacks evening access?~Baseline;Daytime data;Different service period;Obtain relevant evening evidence | defining the problem as needing a particular vendor or program | Fictional council analyst Mira is asked to justify a predetermined policy using evidence from another population.')
add('GPP','policy-option-comparison | Policy option comparison | Policy objective;Candidate interventions;Decision criteria and evidence | Include a baseline option representing current practice~Describe the mechanism and implementation requirements of each option~Compare options against the same stated criteria~Separate evidence-supported effects from assumptions~Identify tradeoffs and the evidence that would change the ranking | criteria weights are disputed=>show separate stakeholder weightings=>a single hidden weighting masks value choices~one option lacks evidence=>mark uncertainty rather than score it as zero benefit=>absence of measurement is not proof of no effect | Option;Mechanism;Tradeoff;Evidence gap | Fictional town Cedarfield compares extending opening hours with simplifying appointments for a public service. | Longer hours;More time access;Staff coverage needed;Demand by time unknown~Simpler appointments;Less booking friction;May not solve time constraints;Booking failure evidence needed | ranking options using different criteria for each favorite | Fictional analyst Noor receives two options and asks for a recommendation without an agreed objective.')
add('GPP','policy-theory-of-change-map | Policy theory of change map | Proposed intervention;Target outcome;Implementation assumptions | Map activities to immediate outputs before naming longer-term outcomes~Specify the behavioral or institutional link between each stage~Identify external conditions needed for the link to hold~Add observable indicators for both implementation and outcomes~Mark the links with weakest evidence as test priorities | an output is presented as the final outcome=>separate delivery from effect=>completed activity does not establish benefit~a causal link has no plausible mechanism=>revise the chain=>sequence alone does not explain change | Chain link;Proposed mechanism;Assumption;Observation | Fictional program Fernbridge distributes service guides to improve application completion. | Guide delivery to use;Applicants read guidance;Guides reach intended users;Check receipt and use~Use to completion;Instructions reduce confusion;Other barriers manageable;Track unresolved application barriers | treating distributed materials as proof of improved outcomes | Fictional program lead Eli claims that holding workshops proves participants changed their behavior.')
add('GPP','policy-stakeholder-impact-map | Policy stakeholder impact map | Proposed policy change;Affected groups;Evidence of benefits and burdens | Identify groups by how the policy changes their choices or access~Separate direct users from implementers and indirectly affected people~Map each group specific benefit, burden and uncertainty~Check whether aggregate benefits conceal concentrated disadvantages~Prepare consultation questions for groups whose experience is missing | a group is absent from existing feedback=>mark its perspective unknown=>silence is not consent~benefits and burdens fall on different groups=>show the distribution explicitly=>a total score can hide the tradeoff | Group;Potential change;Evidence status;Consultation question | Fictional town Alderport plans online-only booking and has feedback only from frequent internet users. | Frequent online users;Potential convenience;Feedback available;Which steps remain difficult?~Residents with access barriers;Potential exclusion;Perspective missing;What alternative access is needed? | interpreting lack of complaints as lack of burden | Fictional analyst Mae must assess a service change using feedback only from current successful users.')
add('GPP','policy-implementation-bottleneck-review | Policy implementation bottleneck review | Policy delivery process;Observed delays;Responsible actors | Map the delivery sequence from eligibility intake to completed service~Place observed queues or failures at specific handoffs~Distinguish demand pressure from capacity, rework and unclear ownership~Identify which constraints affect downstream throughput~Propose a small diagnostic change with a measurable observation | the same work repeatedly returns upstream=>investigate rework cause=>adding capacity alone may preserve the loop~delay evidence lacks timestamps=>collect stage observations before assigning blame=>total duration does not identify the bottleneck | Stage;Observed symptom;Possible constraint;Diagnostic action | Fictional service Birchgate has applications returned repeatedly for unclear attachments. | Intake review;Repeated returns;Instruction ambiguity possible;Inspect rejection reasons~Final processing;Late starts;Upstream rework dependency;Track arrival after corrections | blaming the slowest visible office without tracing upstream rework | Fictional policy team sees long total turnaround but has no stage-level observations.')
add('GPP','policy-indicator-definition-sheet | Policy indicator definition sheet | Policy objective;Available records;Reporting audience | Translate the objective into observable constructs~Define each indicator numerator or count, population, period and unit~State exclusions and missing-data handling before calculation~Distinguish process measures from outcome measures~Prepare a calculation note and interpretation limits for each indicator | the denominator changes across periods=>report the change before comparing rates=>apparent improvement may reflect different coverage~an indicator measures activity only=>label it as process evidence=>activity is not the same as policy impact | Indicator;Operational definition;Data limit;Interpretation | Fictional office Willowmere counts completed requests but does not record requests abandoned before submission. | Completed requests;Recorded completions in stated period;Abandonment missing;Describes completed workload~Access outcome;Not yet operationalized;Unrecorded unsuccessful users;Needs broader observation | labeling completed case counts as universal access | Fictional analyst Sam compares offices using raw counts despite different service populations.')
add('GPP','public-consultation-response-coding | Public consultation response coding | Consultation submissions;Consultation questions;Coding purpose | Remove identifying details unnecessary for thematic analysis~Create a codebook distinguishing proposals, reasons and experiences~Apply multiple codes where a submission contains distinct points~Track minority or contradictory themes without reducing them to noise~Summarize themes with source references and limits on representativeness | repeated identical submissions appear=>record repetition and unique arguments separately=>volume and diversity answer different questions~respondents are self-selected=>avoid population prevalence claims=>submission shares are not a representative survey | Theme;Supporting material;Counterpoint;Interpretation limit | Fictional consultation Oakwell receives repeated template responses favoring a change and a detailed access concern. | Support for change;Repeated template text;Unique reasoning limited;Do not infer population support~Access concern;Detailed individual account;No prevalence data;Preserve as issue to investigate | treating consultation frequency as an election result | Fictional analyst Jules is asked to call a proposal universally popular from self-selected submissions.')
add('GPP','policy-evidence-brief | Policy evidence brief | Policy question;Included studies and official data;Decision deadline | Define the specific decision the evidence can inform~Extract study context, design and outcome rather than only conclusions~Group evidence by relevance and evidential limitations~Separate consistent findings from unresolved disagreement~Write a brief that states what the evidence supports and what remains a value or implementation choice | studies concern different interventions=>avoid pooling their conclusions as one effect=>the policy mechanisms may differ~evidence is indirect=>state the transfer assumption=>another setting may not reproduce the result | Evidence item;Relevance;Limitation;Decision implication | Fictional analyst Rin reviews a pilot in a small office for a proposed regional rollout. | Pilot report;Similar service task;Different scale;Suggests questions for rollout~Regional records;Local demand context;No intervention comparison;Informs implementation planning | saying research proves success without examining study context | Fictional analyst Tessa has one pilot report and asks for a definitive region-wide effect claim.')
add('GPP','policy-scenario-stress-test | Policy scenario stress test | Proposed policy design;Uncertain drivers;Acceptable performance conditions | Identify uncertainties that can change implementation or outcomes~Build internally consistent contrasting scenarios without assigning unsupported probabilities~Trace how the policy behaves in each scenario~Identify failure points and actions that remain useful across scenarios~Define observable signals that would trigger a design review | a scenario is only a preferred forecast=>add a plausible adverse condition=>stress testing requires exposure to failure~an action helps in all tested scenarios=>consider it a robust candidate=>its value is less dependent on one prediction | Scenario;Policy pressure;Failure signal;Adaptation | Fictional program Meadowford depends on partner staffing and tests stable staffing against staff turnover. | Stable staffing;Routine delivery;Service targets still need observation;Monitor baseline~Staff turnover;Training and handoff pressure;Unassigned cases;Prepare documented handoffs | treating scenario narratives as probability forecasts | Fictional planner Hugo supplies only an optimistic future and asks whether the policy is resilient.')
add('GPP','policy-review-question-agenda | Policy review question agenda | Policy review purpose;Known evidence gaps;Decision-maker roles | Identify decisions that the review must enable~Convert each uncertainty into an answerable question~Match questions to evidence sources and responsible contributors~Separate questions needing empirical evidence from those needing a value choice~Sequence the agenda so prerequisites precede final recommendations | a question cannot change any decision=>deprioritize it=>review effort should support the stated purpose~a normative choice is disguised as a data gap=>name the value tradeoff=>additional measurement alone will not settle it | Review question;Decision affected;Evidence or judgment;Owner role | Fictional review team Pineharbor must decide whether to expand a service but disagrees about prioritizing coverage or intensity. | Coverage versus intensity;Expansion design;Value choice;Decision-makers~Current unmet demand;Scale of expansion;Empirical evidence;Analysis team | commissioning more data to avoid a stated value disagreement | Fictional review chair Lian asks for an agenda of interesting questions with no connection to upcoming decisions.')

domain('GED','Lesson and Learning Design','Education & Teaching','Design instruction around observable learning goals, prerequisite knowledge, practice, and evidence of understanding.',
       'Treat the learner performance as the target: identify what the learner should be able to do, the conditions under which they should do it, and what would count as a successful attempt. Use supplied curriculum and learner work as the factual basis. Do not infer a diagnosis or fixed ability from one error. Match examples and practice to the actual goal, then use a small check to decide whether to advance, explain differently, or revisit a prerequisite. Label teaching choices as design choices rather than guaranteed learning effects.')
add('GED','learning-objective-rewrite | Observable learning objective rewrite | Existing learning aims;Learner context;Assessment constraints | Replace vague verbs with an observable learner action~Specify the content boundary and conditions of performance~Separate multiple unrelated performances into distinct objectives~Identify evidence that would demonstrate each objective~Check whether the planned assessment can actually elicit that evidence | an objective says understand=>ask what observable action would show understanding=>the verb alone cannot guide assessment~the assessment tests a different action=>revise the objective or assessment=>alignment requires matching the intended performance | Original aim;Observable action;Evidence;Boundary | Fictional teacher Mira wants students to understand arguments and currently plans only a definition quiz. | Understand arguments;Identify claim and supporting reason;Annotated short argument;Use supplied text~Definition quiz;Recall a term;Insufficient for analysis goal;Add an argument task | replacing a vague verb with another vague verb | Fictional teacher Leo wants learners to appreciate science but cannot describe what they should do afterward.')
add('GED','prerequisite-knowledge-check | Prerequisite knowledge check | Target learning task;Learner work samples;Assumed prior knowledge | Decompose the target task into required component actions~Identify which components must already be available to attempt the task~Create short checks that isolate those components~Interpret errors by component rather than by overall score alone~Choose a brief prerequisite activity or advance decision for each pattern | a check requires the new target skill=>redesign it to isolate prior knowledge=>otherwise it cannot diagnose a prerequisite~one response is ambiguous=>ask a follow-up probe=>guessing can mimic knowledge or error | Prerequisite;Check task;Observed pattern;Instructional response | Fictional tutor Asha teaches paragraph synthesis and sees that a learner can find facts but cannot distinguish a claim from an example. | Fact location;Point to stated detail;Successful;Retain as available prerequisite~Claim distinction;Label claim and example;Confused;Practice contrast before synthesis | calling every wrong answer a lack of effort | Fictional tutor Eli wants a prerequisite check that accidentally includes all steps of the new lesson.')
add('GED','worked-example-lesson-sequence | Worked example lesson sequence | Target procedure;Common learner errors;Practice conditions | Select a complete example that exposes the decisions in the procedure~Annotate why each step is chosen rather than merely naming the operation~Create a partially completed counterpart that requires a meaningful missing decision~Follow with an independent task using changed surface details~Compare learner explanations with the decision logic before increasing difficulty | the example skips a decision=>make that choice visible=>learners cannot infer the intended judgment reliably~practice differs in underlying structure=>label it as transfer work=>it is not simply another repetition | Sequence stage;Learner action;Support;Check | Fictional teacher Noor teaches source comparison using a fully annotated pair of texts and then a new pair. | Annotated example;Explain why sources differ;Decision notes supplied;Learner names comparison basis~Independent pair;Compare new sources;No completed comparison;Check reasoning rather than copied phrases | showing polished answers without revealing the decisions that produced them | Fictional teacher Bea asks for examples that change the numbers but never require a new decision.')
add('GED','misconception-contrast-activity | Misconception contrast activity | Target concept;Observed incorrect reasoning;Correct counterexamples | Describe the incorrect reasoning in the learner own terms~Find a pair of cases where that reasoning predicts different results from the intended concept~Ask the learner to predict and justify before seeing the explanation~Compare the prediction with the defining feature of each case~Check the revised idea with a new case rather than the original example | the supposed misconception is based on one unclear response=>probe before labeling it=>the learner may have misunderstood the question~the contrast changes several features=>simplify it=>the decisive distinction should remain visible | Case;Prediction to elicit;Decisive feature;Follow-up | Fictional tutor Ren hears that longer paragraphs always contain stronger arguments. | Long weak paragraph;Ask whether length proves strength;Unsupported claims;Identify evidence quality~Short supported paragraph;Ask whether brevity makes it weak;Relevant support;Compare reasoning | announcing the correct rule without eliciting the learner prediction | Fictional learner Jo gives an ambiguous answer and the teacher wants a full misconception diagnosis.')
add('GED','lesson-practice-alignment-review | Lesson practice alignment review | Learning objectives;Activities and exercises;Assessment task | Classify what each activity actually asks the learner to do~Map activity demands to the stated objectives~Identify objectives with no practice and activities with no relevant purpose~Check whether practice includes the conditions of the final assessment~Revise the sequence to fill missing practice without adding unrelated volume | an objective has explanation only=>add a learner performance opportunity=>hearing an explanation is not practice evidence~practice supplies support absent from assessment=>include a gradual support reduction=>the assessment demands independent performance | Objective;Current practice;Mismatch;Revision | Fictional course asks learners to critique claims but all exercises ask for term definitions. | Critique claims;Definition exercises;No critique practice;Add claim-evidence analysis~Recall terms;Definition exercises;Aligned but narrower;Retain only needed vocabulary | assuming an engaging activity necessarily practices the objective | Fictional teacher Kai has a group activity but an individual assessment and wants to check the alignment.')
add('GED','scaffold-fading-plan | Scaffold fading plan | Independent target task;Current supports;Learner performance evidence | List the prompts, models and aids currently supplied~Identify which decisions each support performs for the learner~Remove or reduce one support while preserving the target task~Observe whether the learner can supply the missing decision~Restore targeted support or continue fading based on the observed difficulty | removing support changes the learning goal=>redesign the step=>fading should test independence on the intended task~the learner fails only one component=>restore support for that component=>full reversion can obscure available competence | Support;Decision it supplies;Fading trial;Observation | Fictional tutor Lian gives a paragraph outline and sentence starters to a learner who now organizes ideas independently. | Outline;Idea order;Remove outline first;Check sequence remains coherent~Sentence starters;Sentence opening;Retain temporarily;Check later independent phrasing | removing every support at once and calling failure inability | Fictional tutor Sol wants to remove a checklist although the learner still omits one critical step.')
add('GED','lesson-question-sequence | Lesson question sequence | Target concept;Learner starting point;Discussion constraints | Begin with a question that reveals the learner current model~Follow with a question requiring evidence or explanation~Introduce a contrasting case that tests the initial reasoning~Ask the learner to revise or defend the model using the comparison~End with a transfer question in a new context | a question can be answered by guessing a word=>ask for the reason or evidence=>the answer alone may not reveal understanding~discussion time is limited=>preserve the diagnostic and transfer questions=>those show starting and ending reasoning | Question role;Prompt focus;Evidence sought;Follow-up | Fictional teacher Ada discusses reliable sources and wants to move beyond asking whether a source looks professional. | Diagnostic;What makes this source trustworthy?;Current criterion;Ask for evidence~Transfer;Apply criterion to a different source;Use of reasoning;Compare with initial answer | stacking leading questions that merely reveal the desired answer | Fictional teacher Ivo asks a sequence where every answer is yes and wants to assess reasoning.')
add('GED','accessible-learning-material-review | Accessible learning material review | Learning material;Learner access needs;Delivery format | Identify the information learners must perceive and the actions they must perform~Check whether meaning depends solely on color, sound, visual position or an unexplained symbol~Provide equivalent text or alternative representations for essential information~Simplify navigation and instructions without removing the learning challenge~Test the revised material against the stated access need and record unresolved constraints | a simplification removes the target reasoning=>restore the intellectual task while changing access=>accessibility is not automatic reduction of the goal~a learner need is unknown=>ask about the barrier rather than assuming a diagnosis=>the useful adaptation depends on actual access | Material element;Access barrier;Revision;Check | Fictional teacher Vera uses color alone to distinguish two categories in a worksheet. | Category labels;Color-only distinction;Add text labels;Meaning survives grayscale~Instructions;Dense paragraph;Separate actions;Learner can locate next step | assuming one format is accessible to every learner | Fictional course designer Omar asks for accessible materials without identifying the delivery format or known barriers.')
add('GED','project-learning-milestone-design | Project learning milestone design | Project outcome;Learning objectives;Available time and resources | Separate the final product from the learning performances it should reveal~Break the project into milestones that produce reviewable evidence~Place feedback before decisions become difficult to revise~Define individual contribution evidence where the project is collaborative~Create a completion criterion for each milestone tied to the learning objective | a milestone is merely time elapsed=>replace it with an inspectable artifact=>progress needs evidence~the final product can hide individual learning=>add individual explanation or reflection=>group output alone may not show each learner performance | Milestone;Artifact;Learning evidence;Feedback decision | Fictional class builds an exhibit but the teacher wants evidence of source evaluation. | Source selection;Annotated source list;Reasons for inclusion;Revise weak sources~Exhibit draft;Claims linked to sources;Use of evaluated evidence;Check unsupported claims | grading only the polished final product | Fictional teacher Pia wants project checkpoints that reveal learning before presentation day.')
add('GED','learning-reflection-prompt-design | Learning reflection prompt design | Completed learner work;Target learning process;Feedback history | Anchor reflection in a specific attempt rather than general feelings~Ask the learner to identify a decision and its evidence~Compare the attempt with a criterion or earlier feedback~Prompt a concrete revision or next experiment~Check whether the proposed change addresses the observed issue | the response is only self-praise or self-criticism=>ask for a specific example=>evaluation needs an observable basis~the next step is vague=>request an action and a check=>intent alone does not guide revision | Reflection focus;Evidence prompt;Revision prompt;Check | Fictional learner Finn says an essay was bad but cannot identify why. | Decision evidence;Which claim lacked support?;Add relevant evidence;Check claim-source link~Process change;Where did drafting stall?;Try outline before drafting;Compare next attempt | asking learners to rate themselves without examining work | Fictional teacher Nia wants reflection questions that lead to a specific improvement instead of generic comments.')

domain('GAS','Learning Assessment','Education & Teaching','Design and interpret learning assessments using explicit criteria, evidence, and fair task conditions.',
       'Assess the stated performance rather than unrelated fluency, presentation polish, or access to outside help. Define criteria before judging the work, and preserve examples of what each criterion looks like. Separate an observed response from an inference about the learner. When a task is revised, check whether the construct being assessed has also changed. Use feedback that points to a repairable feature of the work. Do not claim that a score has a level of precision or comparability that the task design and scoring evidence do not support.')
add('GAS','analytic-rubric-design | Analytic rubric design | Learning performance;Sample work;Assessment constraints | Separate the performance into distinct criteria~Define observable differences between performance levels for each criterion~Avoid descriptors that only change adjectives such as good or excellent~Test the rubric against contrasting work samples~Revise criteria that overlap or produce unexplained disagreement | two criteria reward the same feature=>merge or differentiate them=>double counting distorts the judgment~a level descriptor names effort only=>replace it with work evidence=>effort is not directly visible in the submitted product | Criterion;Observable distinction;Sample evidence;Revision | Fictional teacher Esme assesses explanations and has two criteria both rewarding detailed writing. | Reasoning;Connects claim to evidence;Link present or absent;Keep separate from length~Detail;Currently duplicates reasoning;No distinct evidence;Redefine or remove | assigning levels using only vague praise words | Fictional teacher Remy wants a rubric whose top level says excellent and lower level says poor.')
add('GAS','assessment-task-validity-review | Assessment task validity review | Intended construct;Assessment prompt;Allowed conditions | Identify the exact capability the task is meant to reveal~List additional demands introduced by the prompt or format~Check whether success can occur without the target capability~Check whether irrelevant barriers can prevent a capable learner from demonstrating it~Revise the task and state remaining limits on interpretation | the task rewards memorized wording while aiming at transfer=>change the stimulus or required explanation=>recall alone does not demonstrate transfer~reading complexity is unrelated to the construct=>reduce that barrier=>otherwise scores partly reflect an unintended demand | Task demand;Relation to construct;Risk;Revision | Fictional teacher Dara wants to assess causal reasoning but uses a dense unfamiliar passage. | Causal explanation;Target demand;Relevant;Retain reasoning question~Unfamiliar vocabulary;Incidental demand;May obscure reasoning;Clarify unnecessary terms | claiming a task measures only its intended skill without checking other demands | Fictional assessor Bo wants to infer problem-solving ability from a task dominated by unfamiliar terminology.')
add('GAS','multiple-choice-item-review | Multiple-choice item review | Item stem and options;Learning objective;Answer rationale | Check that the stem presents a complete and focused problem~Verify the keyed answer against the supplied teaching source~Ensure distractors reflect plausible errors without becoming defensible alternatives~Remove grammatical or length cues that reveal the key~Prepare a rationale explaining why each option is right or wrong | more than one option is defensible=>revise the stem or options=>the score would otherwise depend on guessing the writer intent~a distractor is absurd=>replace it with a relevant misconception=>it contributes little diagnostic information | Item feature;Observed issue;Revision;Rationale check | Fictional item asks for the best source but gives no evaluation criterion, making two options plausible. | Stem;Best undefined;State criterion;Key follows stated purpose~Options;Two defensible answers;Revise overlap;Explain each rejection | making the correct option longer and more qualified than every distractor | Fictional teacher Faye supplies an item with two plausible keys and asks you to pick the intended one.')
add('GAS','short-answer-scoring-guide | Short-answer scoring guide | Short-answer prompt;Expected reasoning;Possible valid alternatives | Identify the essential elements that answer the question~Separate required reasoning from optional wording~Describe partially correct responses by the elements present~List common errors and how they affect the judgment~Test the guide on paraphrases and alternative valid approaches | a response uses different wording but equivalent reasoning=>credit the demonstrated element=>the target is meaning rather than phrase matching~the prompt permits several methods=>include those routes=>a single model answer may be too narrow | Required element;Acceptable evidence;Partial response;Scoring note | Fictional teacher Noel asks learners to explain a source conflict and has a guide requiring one exact phrase. | Conflict identification;Meaningful paraphrase;Names disagreement only;Credit identified element~Reasoning;Explains context difference;No explanation;Do not require fixed wording | treating a model answer as the only valid wording | Fictional scorer Arin receives a correct alternative explanation absent from the initial answer key.')
add('GAS','formative-exit-ticket-design | Formative exit ticket design | Lesson objective;Likely learner errors;Next lesson decision | Choose the single teaching decision the exit ticket should inform~Write a short task that distinguishes the relevant understanding patterns~Define response categories before collecting answers~Assign an instructional response to each category~Check that the task can be completed under the actual classroom conditions | the response will not change instruction=>revise the ticket purpose=>collection alone is not formative use~a question invites only confidence ratings=>add performance evidence=>confidence may differ from understanding | Response pattern;Evidence task;Interpretation;Next teaching action | Fictional teacher Mina needs to know whether learners distinguish a claim from its evidence. | Correct distinction;Label claim and support;Ready for comparison;Use new argument~Reversed labels;Same task;Concept distinction unclear;Use contrast example | collecting tickets without deciding what different answers will trigger | Fictional teacher Uri asks for an exit ticket but plans identical next steps regardless of responses.')
add('GAS','learner-error-pattern-analysis | Learner error pattern analysis | Learner responses;Task demands;Correct reasoning | Group errors by the decision or concept involved~Separate omissions, transcription mistakes and conceptual explanations~Compare patterns across tasks before inferring a stable difficulty~Choose a follow-up probe that distinguishes competing explanations~Recommend a focused teaching response based on the probe outcome | an error appears only once=>treat it as a hypothesis=>one response cannot establish a persistent pattern~several errors share one prerequisite=>target that prerequisite=>repeating the full task may not address the cause | Error pattern;Possible explanation;Discriminating probe;Response | Fictional learner Sia labels evidence as a claim in one item but succeeds in a simpler item. | Reversed labels;Complexity or concept issue;Use comparable simpler text;Check consistency~Correct simple item;Some distinction available;Increase one demand only;Locate difficulty | diagnosing a fixed inability from a single answer | Fictional teacher Ren wants to label a learner weak from one incomplete response.')
add('GAS','feedback-comment-rewrite | Actionable feedback rewrite | Learner work;Assessment criteria;Existing comments | Identify the exact feature each comment addresses~Link the observation to the relevant criterion~Replace general praise or criticism with specific evidence~Give a feasible revision action while preserving learner authorship~Prioritize comments that affect the main learning goal | a comment supplies the entire answer=>convert it to a revision prompt=>feedback should support the learner next decision~comments conflict=>resolve priority before delivery=>the learner cannot follow incompatible directions | Original comment;Work evidence;Actionable revision;Success check | Fictional teacher Jae writes unclear beside an unsupported claim. | Unclear;Claim lacks supporting source;Add evidence and explain its link;Reader can trace support~Good work;Comparison names both sources;State why contrast matters;Reasoning becomes explicit | replacing all learner reasoning with a polished teacher rewrite | Fictional teacher Ana has many comments but none says what the learner should change.')
add('GAS','assessment-moderation-session | Assessment moderation session | Rubric;Anonymized work samples;Independent judgments | Ask reviewers to judge the same sample before discussing it~Compare criterion-level differences rather than only total judgments~Require each reviewer to cite evidence from the work~Clarify ambiguous rubric wording using the disagreement~Recheck another sample to see whether the clarification improves consistency | disagreement concerns the criterion meaning=>revise the descriptor=>averaging judgments does not resolve interpretation~reviewers saw different information=>equalize the evidence=>comparison requires a common basis | Disagreement;Work evidence;Rubric issue;Resolution | Fictional reviewers Inez and Pavel disagree about whether an essay explains evidence or only lists it. | Explanation criterion;Evidence listed without link;Descriptor ambiguous;Define what a link looks like~Overall judgment;Different criterion readings;Not yet comparable;Rejudge after clarification | averaging scores without investigating why reviewers differ | Fictional moderation team has identical totals but opposite criterion judgments.')
add('GAS','portfolio-evidence-selection | Learning portfolio evidence selection | Portfolio purpose;Available work;Learning criteria | Map each criterion to possible evidence artifacts~Choose artifacts that show the learner contribution and process~Include a revision pair where change is relevant to the purpose~Add a short annotation explaining what each artifact demonstrates~Identify criteria still unsupported instead of filling space with unrelated work | an artifact is impressive but irrelevant=>exclude or reframe it=>portfolio size is not evidence coverage~group work hides contribution=>add individual evidence=>the portfolio must support claims about the learner | Criterion;Artifact;Evidence claim;Gap | Fictional learner Kira has a polished group poster and an earlier individual draft for a writing portfolio. | Individual reasoning;Individual draft;Shows own argument choices;Needs revision evidence~Presentation polish;Group poster;Contribution unclear;Add role evidence or omit | selecting only attractive artifacts regardless of learning criteria | Fictional learner Theo wants a portfolio made entirely of final group products.')
add('GAS','assessment-results-communication | Assessment results explanation | Results and scoring basis;Audience;Interpretation limits | Explain what the assessment asked the learner to demonstrate~Describe strengths using observed work evidence~Identify the most consequential next learning need~State limits on comparisons or conclusions from the result~Provide a practical next action linked to that need | results come from different tasks=>avoid direct ranking without comparability evidence=>the tasks may demand different performances~a score is presented without context=>add task and criteria information=>a number alone cannot guide improvement | Finding;Observed evidence;Interpretation limit;Next step | Fictional learner Lio identifies claims accurately but struggles to explain support in a short assessment. | Claim identification;Correct labels in supplied work;Limited task sample;Retain and apply in new text~Evidence explanation;Links missing;Does not imply general inability;Practice explicit reasoning links | converting one assessment result into a fixed label about ability | Fictional parent Dana asks whether one low result proves a child cannot learn the subject.')

domain('GEX','Exam Preparation Methods','Exam Prep','Plan exam preparation from current exam requirements, performance evidence, and available study capacity.',
       'Use the official current syllabus, format and permitted materials as inputs rather than reproducing them from memory. Separate learning a topic from performing it under exam conditions. Track errors by cause and revisit whether practice is actually changing performance. Treat practice scores as evidence from particular tasks, not guaranteed predictions of a future result. Protect time for sleep and ordinary breaks without prescribing medical guidance. If the requested plan exceeds available capacity, show the tradeoff explicitly rather than promising that every topic can receive full treatment.')
add('GEX','exam-syllabus-coverage-map | Exam syllabus coverage map | Current official syllabus;Existing study materials;Learner confidence evidence | Break syllabus statements into assessable tasks~Map each task to available learning and practice resources~Mark coverage separately from demonstrated competence~Identify missing or outdated materials against the current official source~Prioritize unresolved coverage gaps for resource selection | a resource title matches a topic but lacks relevant practice=>mark partial coverage=>a matching label does not establish preparation~syllabus currency is unknown=>verify the official version=>an old outline may omit current requirements | Syllabus task;Resource coverage;Performance evidence;Gap action | Fictional candidate Mira has notes for a topic but no practice requiring explanation. | Topic explanation;Notes available;No demonstrated response;Find aligned practice~Definition recall;Flashcards available;Recall checked;Keep distinct from explanation | marking a topic mastered because it appears in a textbook | Fictional candidate Ravi uses an old syllabus and wants every topic marked complete from chapter titles.')
add('GEX','exam-study-capacity-plan | Exam study capacity plan | Exam date and requirements;Available study blocks;Priority weaknesses | Calculate genuinely available blocks after fixed obligations~Assign high-priority tasks to blocks with suitable concentration demands~Include practice, review and recovery from missed work~Make tradeoffs visible when demand exceeds capacity~Define a weekly adjustment rule based on completed evidence rather than planned hours | required work exceeds available blocks=>reduce scope or renegotiate priorities=>a crowded timetable does not create time~blocks are repeatedly missed=>revise the capacity assumption=>the plan should reflect observed availability | Study demand;Available capacity;Allocation;Adjustment trigger | Fictional candidate Nia has evening blocks but consistently loses one to a fixed family duty. | Core practice;Remaining reliable evenings;Schedule first;Review completion evidence~Optional review;Unreliable block;Move or reduce;Do not double-book | scheduling the same time for multiple subjects | Fictional candidate Leo wants a complete plan that ignores known work commitments.')
add('GEX','exam-practice-error-log | Exam practice error log | Attempted questions;Marked answers;Learner explanations | Record the question task and attempted reasoning~Classify the error as knowledge, method selection, execution or interpretation using evidence~Write a correction that explains the failed decision~Assign a new practice item that tests the same decision with changed details~Review whether the error recurs before closing the log entry | the learner copied the solution without explanation=>keep the entry open=>recognition is not evidence of independent correction~the error category is uncertain=>use a diagnostic reattempt=>the remedy depends on the cause | Error;Evidence of cause;Repair;Retest | Fictional candidate Amir selected a familiar method without checking the question condition. | Method selection;Ignored condition;State applicability test;Try changed-condition item~Execution;No evidence of arithmetic issue;Do not assign arithmetic drills;Observe next attempt | labeling every mistake careless | Fictional candidate Jo repeats an error after memorizing the answer and asks to mark it fixed.')
add('GEX','exam-mock-review | Mock exam review | Mock paper and responses;Timing notes;Official exam format | Compare performance by task type instead of only total score~Locate unanswered work and identify whether time, knowledge or strategy caused it~Review representative errors using the actual attempted reasoning~Select a small set of changes for the next mock~Define what evidence will show whether each change helped | timing notes are absent=>avoid precise pacing conclusions=>total duration cannot locate where time was lost~a score changed on a different paper=>compare task demands before claiming improvement=>paper difficulty may differ | Pattern;Evidence;Change to test;Next-mock check | Fictional candidate Tess leaves a long response blank after spending excessive time polishing an earlier answer. | Incomplete long response;Blank response and timing note;Set a move-on rule;Check coverage next mock~Early polishing;Repeated revisions;Draft sufficient answer first;Review time use | celebrating a score change without checking paper comparability | Fictional candidate Eli has only a total mock score and asks which exact topic caused the loss.')
add('GEX','exam-retrieval-practice-design | Retrieval practice set | Target knowledge;Common confusions;Available source notes | Convert source content into questions requiring recall before viewing answers~Mix prompts that distinguish closely related concepts~Write answer keys that preserve essential meaning and acceptable alternatives~Separate failed retrieval from uncertain source accuracy~Schedule a later reattempt with changed prompt wording | a prompt reveals most of the answer=>remove the cue=>the task should require retrieval~the source itself is uncertain=>verify it before making a key=>practice should not reinforce an error | Prompt type;Knowledge target;Answer criterion;Reattempt | Fictional candidate Uma confuses a claim with an example and uses cards that show both labels. | Contrast prompt;Claim versus example;Correct distinction plus reason;Use new paragraph~Free recall;Definition meaning;Essential idea in own words;Change wording | treating rereading an exposed answer as successful recall | Fictional candidate Max calls a card mastered after recognizing its answer while the answer remains visible.')
add('GEX','exam-interleaved-practice-selection | Mixed practice selection | Related problem types;Learner method knowledge;Practice bank | Identify problem types that require choosing among methods~Confirm the learner has an initial example of each method~Select a mixed set where the method is not labeled in advance~Require a brief method-choice justification before solving~Review selection errors separately from execution errors | the learner has never seen a method=>provide an initial worked example=>mixed selection cannot substitute for all initial instruction~items differ only in surface wording=>include genuinely different method choices=>otherwise mixing does not test selection | Practice item;Method choice required;Reason to state;Review focus | Fictional candidate Hana knows two solution approaches but chooses by the appearance of keywords. | Unlabeled mixed item;Choose applicable approach;Name governing condition;Selection reasoning~Same approach new wording;Recognize structure;Ignore misleading keyword;Transfer of choice | labeling each practice section with the method to use | Fictional candidate Sol wants mixed practice but has not learned one of the included methods.')
add('GEX','exam-answer-structure-practice | Exam answer structure practice | Question command;Marking criteria if supplied;Candidate draft | Identify the action demanded by the question command in its actual context~Extract the content boundaries and required comparison or explanation~Outline an answer with each section serving a criterion~Check that evidence and reasoning support the stated conclusion~Practice a concise version that fits the verified response constraints | the command is ambiguous=>use the official guidance or supplied rubric=>ordinary synonyms may not reflect exam expectations~the draft contains irrelevant knowledge=>remove it from the core answer=>coverage should serve the question | Answer part;Question demand;Draft issue;Revision | Fictional candidate Aria is asked to compare two proposals but writes separate descriptions without a comparison basis. | Comparison basis;Shared criteria needed;Missing;Choose relevant common criteria~Conclusion;Judgment from comparison;Unsupported preference;Tie it to stated criteria | dumping all memorized facts regardless of the question | Fictional candidate Ben answers an explain question with a list and wants structural feedback.')
add('GEX','exam-time-allocation-rehearsal | Exam time allocation rehearsal | Verified exam structure;Practice timing records;Completion priorities | Identify task types and constraints from the current official format~Use practice records to estimate where time is actually spent~Choose provisional move-on checkpoints rather than rigid guesses~Rehearse the plan on a comparable practice set~Revise allocations using completion and answer-quality evidence | a task repeatedly overruns=>inspect the cause before shrinking all other tasks=>method difficulty and perfectionism require different repairs~official rules restrict navigation=>build those restrictions into rehearsal=>an unrestricted practice plan may not transfer | Task stage;Observed timing issue;Provisional rule;Rehearsal evidence | Fictional candidate Ivo can revisit questions but spends too long perfecting early responses. | First pass;Over-polishing;Move after sufficient draft;Check later-question coverage~Review pass;No time remains;Reserve review only after coverage;Compare answer quality | promising a universal number of minutes for every question | Fictional candidate Fia wants timing advice without giving the exam structure or practice observations.')
add('GEX','exam-final-review-triage | Final review triage | Remaining time;Error log;Current exam scope | Identify unresolved errors likely to affect multiple tasks~Separate repairable weaknesses from topics requiring extensive new learning~Choose review tasks that produce observable improvement evidence~Preserve essential logistics checks and avoid expanding the syllabus~Create a stop rule for low-yield repetition using the candidate stated priorities | a topic is wholly new and time is short=>make the opportunity cost explicit=>covering it may displace repairable recurring errors~review repeats already reliable tasks=>shift to unresolved evidence=>comfort is not the same as preparation need | Review target;Evidence;Priority reason;Completion check | Fictional candidate Noor has little remaining time and repeatedly misreads question conditions while already recalling definitions reliably. | Condition reading;Recurring error log;Affects several tasks;Correctly annotate new prompts~Definition recall;Reliable attempts;Lower marginal need;Brief maintenance only | adding an entire new resource collection at the final stage | Fictional candidate Jan wants to reread everything despite a clear recurring error pattern.')
add('GEX','exam-day-logistics-checklist | Exam-day logistics checklist | Official candidate instructions;Travel or access plan;Permitted materials | Extract arrival, identification and permitted-material requirements from current official instructions~Separate confirmed facts from assumptions about the venue or platform~Prepare materials without including prohibited or unverified items~Create a contingency contact plan using official support information~Have the candidate check the final list against the latest notice | instructions changed after an earlier notice=>compare the current notice and confirm conflicts=>an old checklist may no longer apply~a required item is unavailable=>contact the official support route=>invented substitutes may not be accepted | Logistics item;Official basis;Readiness;Action | Fictional candidate Edda has a venue notice but relies on a friend description of accepted identification. | Identification;Friend description only;Unverified;Check official candidate instructions~Venue;Official notice supplied;Address available;Confirm travel plan | treating advice from another candidate as authoritative instructions | Fictional candidate Kai asks you to guarantee entry using an identification substitute not mentioned in the official notice.')

domain('GRQ','Research Question and Study Design','Research Methods & Academia','Define answerable research questions, sampling decisions, measurements, and feasible study protocols.',
       'Distinguish a study design proposal from completed research. Define the unit being studied and the comparison needed before naming a method. Record feasibility, consent, privacy and institutional review requirements relevant to the actual setting, using current institutional guidance instead of assumed exemptions. Do not invent recruitment access, instruments, prior findings, sample-size justifications or causal identification. A design can be useful while a required decision remains open; state exactly which conclusion the missing decision prevents. Use only fictional participant details in examples.', 'medium')
add('GRQ','research-question-narrowing | Research question narrowing | Broad research interest;Available population or materials;Feasibility constraints | Separate the topic from the question to be answered~Specify the unit of analysis and setting~Identify the outcome or phenomenon requiring observation~Choose a comparison or descriptive boundary that makes the question answerable~State what the proposed evidence could and could not establish | the question contains several independent outcomes=>split it into primary and secondary questions=>one unfocused question makes design choices unclear~required data are inaccessible=>revise scope before choosing analysis=>a method cannot compensate for unavailable observations | Question element;Proposed scope;Evidence needed;Limit | Fictional researcher Mara wants to study remote learning but can access only one course discussion archive. | Population;One course archive;Archived contributions;Cannot represent all students~Phenomenon;Participation patterns;Defined contribution measures;Cannot infer motives alone | selecting a fashionable method before defining the question | Fictional researcher Eli wants to prove a broad social claim using a small convenience archive.')
add('GRQ','research-construct-operationalization | Construct operationalization map | Research construct;Study context;Candidate observations | Define the construct in plain language before selecting measures~List observable indicators that could represent different aspects~Identify plausible alternative explanations for each indicator~Specify collection conditions and coding rules~State which aspects remain unmeasured | a convenient proxy omits a central aspect=>label partial coverage=>availability does not establish construct equivalence~different indicators disagree=>retain the disagreement for investigation=>averaging can hide distinct dimensions | Construct aspect;Indicator;Alternative explanation;Interpretation boundary | Fictional researcher Noor studies engagement using attendance records alone. | Presence;Attendance record;Required attendance may explain presence;Not full engagement~Active contribution;Not yet observed;Access barriers may affect visibility;Needs separate measure | renaming an available count as the entire construct | Fictional researcher Tia equates message volume with motivation without considering context.')
add('GRQ','study-design-question-fit | Study design question fit | Research question;Available evidence;Desired inference | Identify whether the question is descriptive, comparative, explanatory or exploratory~Specify the evidence needed for the intended inference~Compare feasible designs against that evidence requirement~List threats each design leaves unresolved~Choose a provisional design and state the inference boundary | the user asks for causality from an uncontrolled observation=>limit the claim or strengthen design=>association alone leaves alternative explanations~the purpose is exploratory=>allow open-ended evidence collection=>premature fixed categories may exclude relevant findings | Design option;Question fit;Unresolved threat;Permitted claim | Fictional researcher Dev wants to know why attendance changed after a course redesign but has only before-and-after totals. | Before-after description;Shows timing of change;Other changes uncontrolled;Describe association~Additional comparison;Could test alternatives;Comparable group unavailable;Treat as design requirement | calling a temporal sequence proof of causation | Fictional researcher Sol asks for a causal conclusion from one observational snapshot.')
add('GRQ','sampling-plan-rationale | Sampling plan rationale | Target population;Recruitment access;Study purpose | Define the population to which the study question refers~Describe the accessible sampling frame and its exclusions~Choose a selection approach consistent with the intended claim~Identify likely nonparticipation and coverage gaps~Document what the resulting sample can support without inventing representativeness | access is limited to volunteers=>state selection limitations=>volunteers may differ from nonparticipants~the design needs subgroup comparison=>check whether recruitment can support it=>a total sample alone does not guarantee useful subgroup evidence | Sampling element;Proposed approach;Coverage gap;Claim limit | Fictional researcher Alma recruits from an optional workshop while asking about all employees. | Accessible frame;Workshop attendees;Nonattendees excluded;Workshop-based findings only~Participation;Volunteers;Self-selection;Do not claim workforce prevalence | describing a convenient sample as representative without support | Fictional researcher Finn wants population estimates from friends who answer an online form.')
add('GRQ','research-interview-guide | Research interview guide | Research question;Participant experience;Interview constraints | Map each research question to information participants can reasonably provide~Write open prompts about concrete experiences before abstract opinions~Add neutral probes for sequence, meaning and exceptions~Remove leading wording and assumptions about the desired answer~Pilot the guide and record which prompts produce unclear or irrelevant responses | a prompt assumes the outcome=>rewrite it neutrally=>the question should not supply the participant interpretation~a participant lacks the relevant experience=>use a routing question=>forcing an answer creates speculation | Guide component;Actual prompt;Purpose;Probe | Fictional researcher Lena interviews people about a service booking experience without assuming it was difficult. | Opening;Tell me about the last time you tried to book.;Concrete experience;What happened next?~Exception;Was there a time it worked differently?;Variation;What was different about that occasion? | asking participants to confirm the researcher favored explanation | Fictional researcher Arun writes Why was the new process confusing and asks for a neutral replacement.')
add('GRQ','survey-question-wording-review | Survey question wording review | Draft survey items;Construct definitions;Respondent context | Identify the single judgment each item should request~Remove double questions, undefined periods and ambiguous referents~Check response options for overlap and missing applicable answers~Separate factual recall from opinion or frequency estimates~Prepare a small comprehension check before using the item | an item asks about two experiences=>split it=>a single answer cannot show which part it refers to~respondents may lack the information=>include an appropriate unknown route=>forced answers can misrepresent knowledge | Item issue;Observed wording;Revision;Check | Fictional survey asks whether a service was fast and helpful. | Double question;Fast and helpful combined;Ask about speed and helpfulness separately;Can answers differ?~Time reference;No episode specified;Refer to the most recent use;Does respondent identify same event? | polishing grammar while leaving a double-barreled question intact | Fictional researcher Jo asks about frequent use without defining the period or response categories.')
add('GRQ','research-pilot-protocol | Research pilot protocol | Proposed study procedure;Known uncertainties;Pilot resources | List process uncertainties that a pilot can actually resolve~Choose observations that reveal recruitment, comprehension or measurement problems~Define what would trigger a protocol revision~Run through the proposed participant journey using permitted test materials~Document changes and unresolved issues without treating pilot results as definitive findings | pilot participants differ from target participants=>limit conclusions about usability=>the actual group may encounter different barriers~the pilot changes the measure=>record the version change=>later data need a clear instrument history | Pilot question;Observation;Revision trigger;Record | Fictional team Wrenfield pilots a questionnaire to check whether respondents interpret the reference period consistently. | Period comprehension;Participant paraphrase;Different periods inferred;Revise wording~Completion flow;Skipped routing question;Incorrect path followed;Repair instructions | treating a successful dry run as proof of study validity | Fictional researcher Mae wants a pilot that only confirms the materials look professional.')
add('GRQ','research-protocol-decision-log | Research protocol decision log | Draft protocol;Design decisions;Decision rationale and alternatives | Identify decisions that materially affect collection or interpretation~Record the chosen approach and rejected alternatives~Attach the evidence or constraint behind each choice~Distinguish decisions made before seeing results from later changes~Prepare a versioned change record with impact on interpretation | a decision changes after results are seen=>state the timing and reason=>readers need to distinguish planned from exploratory choices~rationale is missing=>record it as unresolved=>a tidy log should not invent historical reasoning | Decision;Rationale;Timing;Interpretation effect | Fictional team Pineglass changes a coding category after encountering unexpected responses. | New category;Unexpected response content;After initial review;Label exploratory change~Original categories;Protocol definition;Before collection;Retain version history | rewriting the protocol as though every later choice was planned | Fictional researcher Neri asks you to backdate a design rationale after observing the results.')
add('GRQ','research-measurement-schedule | Measurement schedule | Study stages;Measures and burden;Expected timing constraints | Map each measure to the research question it serves~Place measures at the points required for interpretation~Identify repeated measures and possible participant burden~Check dependencies such as consent or baseline collection~Create a schedule with missed-measure handling defined in advance | a baseline is collected after exposure=>label it accordingly=>it cannot be treated as a pre-exposure observation~burden exceeds feasible participation=>prioritize essential measures=>an unrealistic schedule threatens completion | Measure;Planned point;Dependency;Missed-measure handling | Fictional team Elmriver plans an initial experience questionnaire but some participants have already used the service. | Pre-use experience;Before service use intended;Already exposed participants differ;Do not label their answers baseline~Follow-up;After specified experience;Contact permission required;Record missing response rather than inventing | treating late-collected information as though collected at the planned time | Fictional researcher Orin wants to fill missing observations using what participants probably would have said.')
add('GRQ','research-feasibility-gate-review | Research feasibility gate review | Research plan;Resources and permissions;Required evidence | Identify the nonnegotiable evidence needed to answer the primary question~Check access, time, skills and permissions against that requirement~Separate solvable implementation gaps from design-breaking constraints~Define a smaller valid study if the original scope is infeasible~Produce a proceed, revise or pause recommendation with explicit conditions | essential access is only assumed=>pause the dependent plan=>a promised dataset or population is not secured access~a smaller scope answers a narrower question=>state the revised question=>feasibility should not silently change the claim | Requirement;Current evidence;Feasibility status;Decision condition | Fictional researcher Vera needs interviews with a restricted group but has no recruitment permission. | Recruitment access;Unconfirmed;Blocking;Obtain permission before scheduling~Alternative archive;Public materials available;Narrower question possible;Revise question before use | solving access problems by pretending a different dataset answers the same question | Fictional researcher Luis has a deadline but no approval to contact the intended participants.')

domain('GRV','Evidence Review and Synthesis','Research Methods & Academia','Find, screen, extract, and synthesize evidence with transparent scope and claim-level support.',
       'Use a reproducible account of what was searched, included and excluded. A source reference must identify a real source that was supplied or actually retrieved; never fabricate authors, titles, dates, quotations or links. Distinguish an author claim from the evidence supporting it. Compare studies only after checking their populations, interventions or phenomena, outcomes, and methods. If evidence is incomplete, describe the bounded search and remaining uncertainty rather than claiming exhaustiveness. Preserve contradictory findings and avoid turning a count of positive studies into an unsupported estimate of effect.', 'medium')
add('GRV','literature-search-concept-map | Literature search concept map | Review question;Known relevant sources;Available databases | Break the question into core concepts and optional context terms~List synonyms and variant terminology from supplied relevant sources~Separate broad discovery terms from narrow screening criteria~Prepare search combinations that can be adapted to each database official syntax~Record test searches and whether known relevant sources are found | a term excludes a known relevant source=>reconsider the concept wording=>overly narrow language can miss relevant evidence~database syntax is uncertain=>check the database help=>guessing operators can change retrieval silently | Concept;Candidate terms;Role;Search check | Fictional reviewer Iris studies service access and has a relevant source using the phrase appointment barriers. | Access;Access and appointment barriers;Core concept;Check known source retrieval~Setting;Named service context;Optional narrowing;Test whether it overrestricts | copying one search syntax into every database without checking support | Fictional reviewer Emil has one jargon term and wants an exhaustive search claim.')
add('GRV','review-inclusion-criteria-design | Review inclusion criteria | Review question;Candidate study types;Scope constraints | Define eligible populations or materials~Specify the phenomenon, intervention or exposure relevant to the question~Define eligible outcomes and evidence types~Separate substantive criteria from convenience limits~Test criteria on borderline examples before screening | a criterion is changed after seeing a favored result=>record the change and reason=>selection rules should not hide outcome-driven choices~an abstract lacks needed information=>mark uncertain for fuller review=>missing detail is not automatic ineligibility | Criterion;Operational rule;Borderline case;Resolution | Fictional reviewer Sam includes studies of service users but encounters a report about staff perceptions only. | Population;Users required;Staff-only report;Exclude unless question is revised~Outcome;User access experience;Abstract unclear;Seek full text | excluding studies because their conclusions are inconvenient | Fictional reviewer Nola wants to include only studies supporting the proposed intervention.')
add('GRV','abstract-screening-decision-log | Abstract screening decision log | Search results;Inclusion criteria;Available abstracts | Apply the same criteria to each record~Record include, exclude or unclear separately~Attach a specific exclusion reason rather than a vague relevance label~Identify duplicates without erasing distinct reports of the same study~Prepare full-text retrieval questions for unclear records | the abstract omits a required detail=>use unclear=>absence from the abstract does not prove the study lacks it~two records describe the same study=>link them=>separate reports may contain complementary information | Record;Screening decision;Criterion basis;Next action | Fictional reviewer Ada sees an abstract with the right population but no stated outcome. | Record A;Unclear;Outcome not described;Retrieve full text~Record B;Exclude;Different population explicitly stated;Record exclusion reason | treating every incomplete abstract as irrelevant | Fictional reviewer Kai asks for a clean included list but refuses to retain uncertain records.')
add('GRV','study-evidence-extraction-sheet | Study evidence extraction sheet | Included source texts;Review question;Extraction fields | Extract population, setting and study design before results~Record the exact outcome definition and observation period~Separate reported results from author interpretation~Attach page or section references to extracted claims~Flag missing or inconsistent information for verification | a result appears only in a discussion claim=>trace it to underlying results=>interpretation alone may overstate the evidence~multiple reports conflict=>preserve both values with sources=>silent correction would hide uncertainty | Extraction field;Source content;Location status;Interpretation note | Fictional reviewer Lea has a report stating satisfaction improved but the supplied excerpt lacks the measure definition. | Outcome definition;Not supplied;Needs methods section;Do not assume scale meaning~Improvement claim;Discussion statement;Results not supplied;Verify supporting observation | copying an abstract conclusion as the entire evidence record | Fictional reviewer Omar asks you to extract an effect size that does not appear in the supplied text.')
add('GRV','research-source-credibility-review | Research source credibility review | Source text;Claim being evaluated;Publication context | Identify the claim the source is being used to support~Check whether the source directly contains relevant evidence~Inspect methods, provenance and disclosed limitations~Distinguish editorial authority from empirical support~State the strongest warranted use and the unresolved checks | a respected outlet reports an unsupported claim=>limit reliance on that claim=>reputation does not replace evidence~a source is preliminary=>label its status=>publication stage affects the available scrutiny | Credibility dimension;Observed evidence;Limit;Use decision | Fictional reviewer Bo reads a polished report with no method description. | Provenance;Named organization supplied;Authority alone insufficient;Check data origin~Methods;Not described;Cannot assess collection;Use only as an attributed claim | treating attractive design as evidence quality | Fictional reviewer Faye wants to cite a report because its cover looks official.')
add('GRV','conflicting-study-findings-map | Conflicting findings map | Studies with differing conclusions;Study context details;Review question | State the apparent disagreement in comparable terms~Compare populations, definitions, measures and designs~Identify whether studies actually answer the same question~Separate methodological explanations from genuine unresolved conflict~Write a synthesis that preserves the remaining disagreement | outcomes differ materially=>do not call results direct contradiction=>the studies may measure different things~the conflict persists after context comparison=>state it explicitly=>choosing a preferred study without rationale conceals uncertainty | Difference;Study comparison;Possible explanation;Synthesis implication | Fictional reviewer Hana compares one report on satisfaction with another on completion rates. | Outcome;Satisfaction versus completion;Different constructs;Not direct contradiction~Setting;Different service offices;Context may matter;Avoid universal conclusion | counting studies on each side without checking comparability | Fictional reviewer Ira asks which study is right although the studies measured different outcomes.')
add('GRV','evidence-gap-map | Evidence gap map | Review scope;Included studies;Decision needs | Define the dimensions that matter to the review question~Place each study in the relevant population, setting and outcome cells~Distinguish empty cells from cells with weak or indirect evidence~Identify gaps that could change a real decision~Prioritize feasible evidence collection without claiming no research exists beyond the search | a cell is empty in a bounded search=>say no included evidence was found=>the search does not prove global absence~a gap cannot affect a decision=>deprioritize it=>novelty alone does not establish usefulness | Evidence cell;Included coverage;Gap type;Decision relevance | Fictional review covers service access studies and finds urban examples but no included rural example. | Urban setting;Included studies available;Quality still needs review;Supports urban-specific questions~Rural setting;No included study;Search-bounded gap;Needed before rural transfer claim | saying no one has ever studied a topic from a limited search | Fictional reviewer Tori wants novelty claims based only on a quick keyword search.')
add('GRV','claim-source-traceability-audit | Claim-source traceability audit | Draft claims;Cited sources;Relevant source excerpts | Break compound claims into individually checkable statements~Locate the source passage that supports each statement~Classify support as direct, partial, indirect or absent~Check that qualifications in the source remain in the draft~Revise or remove unsupported wording without inventing replacement citations | a citation supports only part of a sentence=>split and qualify the sentence=>one reference cannot cover unsupported additions~the source says association and draft says causation=>restore the narrower wording=>the draft exceeds the evidence | Draft claim;Source support;Mismatch;Revision | Fictional writer Ren says a program caused better access while the source reports an association. | Causal claim;Association only;Overstatement;Use associated wording~Universal scope;Single setting;Overgeneralization;Name setting limitation | placing a real citation after a claim it does not support | Fictional writer Ana supplies a citation list but no evidence that the cited sources support the draft.')
add('GRV','narrative-evidence-synthesis | Narrative evidence synthesis | Extracted evidence;Review question;Study differences | Group studies by the question or mechanism they address~Describe the pattern within each group using evidence quality and relevance~Explain important differences before combining conclusions~Separate supported findings from plausible interpretations~End with implications bounded by the included evidence | studies are too heterogeneous for one summary=>report structured subgroups=>a single conclusion would erase meaningful differences~only favorable findings are summarized=>restore relevant contrary evidence=>selective synthesis misrepresents the record | Evidence group;Observed pattern;Important difference;Bounded conclusion | Fictional reviewer Lila has studies of two distinct service designs with different outcome definitions. | Design A;Reported access experiences;Qualitative accounts;Describes user barriers~Design B;Completion records;Different outcome;Do not combine as one effect | turning a collection of studies into a certainty stronger than any supported comparison | Fictional reviewer Eli asks for one confident conclusion despite incompatible outcome definitions.')
add('GRV','review-update-watchlist | Evidence review update watchlist | Existing review scope;Time-sensitive claims;Preferred source channels | Identify conclusions most likely to change with new evidence~List official or primary-source channels relevant to those conclusions~Define what kind of new source would trigger reassessment~Separate a search plan from any actual scheduled monitoring~Prepare an update log that records changed evidence and affected conclusions | monitoring has not been scheduled=>label the output as a plan=>a watchlist is not an active service~new evidence is outside scope=>record it separately=>updates should not silently redefine the review | Claim to revisit;Source channel;Trigger;Affected conclusion | Fictional reviewer Mina maintains a review whose service rules may change and whose empirical findings need later replication. | Administrative route;Responsible agency publication;Updated guidance;Revise process description~Empirical finding;Relevant primary studies;Comparable new evidence;Reassess synthesis | promising to monitor continuously when no monitoring is configured | Fictional researcher Dev asks for a watchlist and assumes you will automatically check it every week.')

domain('GCA','Career Development','Career Development','Translate work evidence and career preferences into applications, interview preparation, and development choices.',
       'Use the person actual experience as the source of claims. Do not invent credentials, employers, outcomes, dates or performance numbers to make an application sound stronger. Distinguish a draft statement from a verified accomplishment, and retain placeholders where the person must confirm details. Current job requirements, hiring processes and platform features should be checked against the employer or platform official material. A career document can present relevant strengths without promising an interview or offer. Drafting outreach does not authorize sending it or sharing the person information.')
add('GCA','career-role-requirement-map | Role requirement evidence map | Job description;Candidate experience;Application constraints | Separate essential requirements from preferences using the actual posting wording~Translate each requirement into evidence a candidate could provide~Map supplied experience to those evidence needs~Distinguish a direct match from transferable experience or an unsupported gap~Prepare application priorities without claiming every requirement is satisfied | a requirement is ambiguous=>retain the wording and ask for clarification if consequential=>inventing a requirement changes the target~experience is transferable but not identical=>state the connection explicitly=>similarity should not be presented as direct experience | Requirement;Candidate evidence;Match type;Application action | Fictional candidate Mira has coordinated volunteer schedules but has not managed paid staff. | Coordination;Volunteer scheduling example;Transferable;Describe actual responsibility~Staff management;No supplied example;Gap;Do not claim direct management | rewriting adjacent experience as a credential the candidate does not hold | Fictional candidate Ren asks you to claim expertise in a tool never used because it appears in the job posting.')
add('GCA','resume-bullet-evidence-rewrite | Resume bullet evidence rewrite | Existing resume bullet;Verified work facts;Target role | Identify the action actually performed by the candidate~Name the task context and contribution boundary~Add a result only when supported by supplied evidence~Remove inflated ownership or unsupported numbers~Write a concise bullet that makes the relevant contribution visible | no measured outcome is available=>describe the observable deliverable=>invented metrics are worse than a precise nonnumeric result~work was collaborative=>state the candidate contribution=>claiming sole ownership misrepresents the work | Bullet element;Verified fact;Draft wording;Check | Fictional candidate Asha organized a shared onboarding checklist with teammates and has no measured time-saving result. | Action;Organized shared checklist;Organized team onboarding steps into a shared checklist.;Confirm ownership wording~Result;Checklist used by team;Supported consistent handoffs with documented responsibilities.;No invented efficiency metric | adding a percentage improvement to every bullet without evidence | Fictional candidate Ivo wants a resume bullet claiming a large efficiency gain with no measurement.')
add('GCA','career-cover-letter-evidence-draft | Cover letter evidence draft | Job posting;Candidate examples;Motivation grounded in supplied facts | Select the role need the letter should address~Choose a concrete candidate example that demonstrates relevant capability~Connect the example to the employer need without copying marketing claims~State motivation using facts the candidate can defend~Draft a short letter with unsupported details left as placeholders | motivation relies on unverified company claims=>ask for a source or use a narrower statement=>flattery should not become invented knowledge~the example does not relate to the role=>choose another or explain transfer=>a letter needs a clear relevance link | Letter part;Evidence basis;Draft text;Review check | Fictional candidate Noor applies for a coordination role and has organized volunteer handoffs. | Opening;Role asks for coordination;I am applying for the coordination role because I enjoy making shared work clear.;Confirm genuine motivation~Evidence paragraph;Volunteer handoff example;In a volunteer project, I organized handoff notes so each person could see the next responsibility.;Keep actual scope | producing generic enthusiasm with no candidate evidence | Fictional candidate Jo asks for a letter praising company achievements that were not supplied or verified.')
add('GCA','interview-story-evidence-bank | Interview story evidence bank | Candidate work episodes;Likely competency questions;Verified outcomes | Select episodes that show distinct decisions or behaviors~Separate context, responsibility, action and result~Identify what the candidate personally did within team work~Add a reflection showing what changed in later practice~Match each story to plausible question themes without memorizing a rigid script | the result is uncertain=>state the observed outcome and limitation=>a story should not invent success~one story is used for every competency=>identify missing evidence=>repetition can conceal a narrow example bank | Story element;Supplied evidence;Speaking point;Boundary | Fictional candidate Leena helped a team recover a missed handoff by clarifying ownership. | Action;Clarified next owner;I listed the pending tasks and asked each owner to confirm the next step.;Team contribution preserved~Reflection;Later checklist introduced;I now check ownership before assuming a handoff is complete.;No invented business result | turning a team accomplishment into a solo rescue narrative | Fictional candidate Max has a useful failure example but asks you to rewrite it as an unqualified success.')
add('GCA','interview-question-practice-plan | Interview question practice plan | Target role;Candidate evidence bank;Known interview format | Identify question types supported by the current employer information~Select questions that test distinct relevant capabilities~Define what a strong answer must demonstrate~Practice a first answer followed by an evidence-seeking probe~Review clarity, relevance and unsupported claims separately | format is unknown=>use a labeled general practice set=>do not claim these are actual employer questions~an answer is polished but unsupported=>return to the evidence bank=>delivery cannot replace substance | Practice question;Capability;Follow-up probe;Review criterion | Fictional candidate Tessa prepares for a coordination role with a handoff example. | Tell me about a missed handoff.;Ownership and repair;What did you personally change?;Specific action~How did you know it improved?;Evidence judgment;What observation supports that?;No invented metric | claiming to know the exact questions an employer will ask | Fictional candidate Amir wants guaranteed interview questions from an employer with no published format.')
add('GCA','career-skill-gap-priority-plan | Career skill gap priority plan | Target work tasks;Current capability evidence;Learning resources and time | Translate the target role into concrete tasks~Compare current independent performance against those tasks~Separate missing knowledge from lack of practice or missing evidence~Prioritize gaps that block several important tasks~Define a small demonstration that would show progress on each gap | the gap is only a missing credential=>check whether the task actually requires it=>credential acquisition and capability development are different decisions~a skill is already present but undocumented=>create evidence rather than unnecessary retraining=>the gap may be visibility | Target task;Current evidence;Gap type;Demonstration | Fictional candidate Ria can organize project notes but has no example showing prioritization under constraints. | Documentation;Existing work sample;Evidence available;Refine sample~Prioritization;No demonstrated tradeoff;Practice and evidence gap;Produce a constrained task plan | buying a course for every keyword in a job advertisement | Fictional candidate Eli lists many tools but cannot identify which work tasks the target role requires.')
add('GCA','career-portfolio-case-study | Portfolio case study draft | Completed project;Candidate contribution;Shareable evidence | Define the project problem and constraints~Describe the candidate role without claiming others work~Select artifacts that show decisions and iteration~Explain the outcome using verified observations~Draft a case study that omits confidential details and names remaining limitations | the project is unfinished=>state the stage=>a prototype should not be presented as a deployed result~confidential evidence is required for a claim=>use an authorized summary or omit it=>portfolio value does not authorize disclosure | Case section;Evidence;Draft statement;Disclosure check | Fictional candidate Nela redesigned a volunteer intake worksheet but has not measured long-term effects. | Problem;Confusing intake notes;The project aimed to make volunteer intake information easier to hand off.;No private records~Outcome;Revised worksheet produced;I produced a revised worksheet and documented the design choices.;No impact claim | presenting a concept mockup as a launched product | Fictional candidate Bo wants to publish client screenshots without confirming permission.')
add('GCA','career-informational-interview-agenda | Informational interview agenda | Career question;Contact role;Known background and time | Identify the decision the conversation should inform~Choose questions the contact experience can reasonably answer~Remove questions answered by readily available official material~Sequence from concrete work experience to advice and reflection~Draft a respectful agenda that does not presume referral or employment | the contact cannot speak for hiring decisions=>ask about experience rather than promises=>their role limits what they can know~the request becomes a job demand=>separate it from the information conversation=>the original purpose is learning | Agenda item;Actual question;Purpose;Boundary | Fictional candidate Mara wants to understand day-to-day coordination work from a practitioner. | Daily work;Which recurring tasks take most of your attention?;Understand reality;Ask about personal experience~Preparation;What would help someone demonstrate readiness?;Identify useful evidence;No referral presumed | treating an informational conversation as an obligation to provide a job | Fictional candidate Quin asks for an agenda that pressures a stranger to refer them immediately.')
add('GCA','career-opportunity-comparison | Career opportunity comparison | Opportunity details;Personal priorities;Unverified terms | Define the candidate own decision criteria~Separate confirmed terms from assumptions or recruiter impressions~Compare opportunities using the same criteria~Identify tradeoffs that cannot be resolved by a single score~Prepare questions that could change the choice | a material term is unconfirmed=>do not treat it as a benefit=>the decision depends on actual terms~priorities conflict=>show the tradeoff explicitly=>a hidden weighting can produce a misleading ranking | Criterion;Opportunity evidence;Tradeoff;Question | Fictional candidate Sora compares a role with confirmed mentoring against one with only a vague promise of flexibility. | Development;Mentoring confirmed in one role;May suit learning priority;What mentoring involves?~Flexibility;Unconfirmed promise;Cannot count as settled;What arrangement is actually offered? | choosing the option with the most attractive unverified promises | Fictional candidate Remy wants a ranking but has not stated whether stability or exploration matters more.')
add('GCA','career-transition-evidence-plan | Career transition evidence plan | Current work history;Target work tasks;Constraints on transition | Identify capabilities shared between current and target work~State where the target context changes the required judgment~Choose a bounded project or practice task that reveals the missing capability~Plan how to describe transferable experience without relabeling it inaccurately~Define a review point based on completed evidence rather than optimism | a transition requires regulated credentials=>check current official requirements=>transferable skill does not replace formal eligibility~the project only repeats current strengths=>add the target-context challenge=>evidence should test the actual gap | Transfer element;Existing evidence;Target difference;Next demonstration | Fictional candidate Lina moves from event coordination toward service operations. | Scheduling;Event schedule experience;Ongoing service dependencies;Create recurring handoff plan~Issue handling;Event exception notes;Repeated-case learning;Analyze recurring causes | claiming the new job title before demonstrating its task demands | Fictional candidate Theo wants to rename past duties to look like a role never held.')

domain('GPR','Personal Work Organization','Personal Productivity','Organize commitments, tasks, attention, and review routines around realistic capacity and observable completion.',
       'Use the person actual commitments and constraints rather than an idealized day. Distinguish a desired outcome, a project, and the next action that can be performed. Keep external promises separate from private intentions, and avoid silently moving a commitment that affects someone else. A plan should show what will be deferred when capacity is insufficient. Treat a routine as an experiment with observable results, not a universal productivity law. Do not turn ordinary organization requests into psychological diagnoses or medical advice.')
add('GPR','personal-task-inbox-triage | Task inbox triage | Unsorted tasks and notes;Current commitments;Available contexts | Separate actionable items from reference material and undecided ideas~Identify the desired outcome for each actionable item~Write the next physical or cognitive action that can begin now~Assign waiting items to the person or event they depend on~Place tasks in a reviewable system without inventing deadlines | an item is too vague to act on=>clarify the intended outcome=>a category label is not a next action~an item depends on someone else=>mark waiting with a follow-up trigger=>it should not appear as immediately executable | Inbox item;Type;Next action;Review trigger | Fictional user Mila lists taxes, call printer and an article idea without supporting detail. | Call printer;Action candidate;Ask about current order status;After response~Article idea;Undecided idea;Store for project review;At chosen review time | treating every captured thought as an urgent commitment | Fictional user Arun wants an action list from vague nouns with no stated outcomes.')
add('GPR','weekly-capacity-planning | Weekly capacity plan | Fixed commitments;Task estimates;Priority outcomes | Block fixed commitments before allocating discretionary work~Estimate capacity using observed availability rather than total waking hours~Select the outcomes that fit the remaining capacity~Reserve explicit room for predictable interruptions or carryover~Name deferred work and the next review point | workload exceeds capacity=>make a deferral or scope tradeoff=>a longer list does not increase available time~estimates are consistently wrong=>revise them from actual records=>planning should learn from experience | Commitment;Capacity effect;Planned handling;Tradeoff | Fictional user Rina has a fixed meeting during the only block originally assigned to drafting. | Fixed meeting;Consumes drafting block;Keep actual commitment;Move or reduce drafting~Drafting;Needs uninterrupted work;Use another confirmed block;Defer optional task | allocating the same hour to several priorities | Fictional user Noel asks for a weekly plan that fits more work than the supplied available time.')
add('GPR','project-next-action-decomposition | Project next-action decomposition | Desired project outcome;Current state;Dependencies | Define what finished means in observable terms~Identify the immediate obstacle between the current state and that outcome~Break the obstacle into a concrete action with available inputs~Separate later actions that depend on the first result~Create a short sequence with a review after uncertain steps | the first action still contains several decisions=>split it further=>the next action should be startable~the outcome is unclear=>clarify completion before decomposition=>otherwise the task list can grow without direction | Project component;Current obstacle;Next action;Dependency | Fictional user Esra wants to organize a workshop but has not chosen a purpose or audience. | Workshop purpose;Undefined;Write intended participant outcome;Before choosing agenda~Venue planning;Requirements unknown;Wait for purpose and audience;Avoid premature booking | decomposing an undefined project into dozens of arbitrary tasks | Fictional user Sam writes finish project as a next action and wants a usable replacement.')
add('GPR','personal-priority-tradeoff | Personal priority tradeoff | Competing tasks;Consequences and commitments;Available capacity | Identify which tasks have genuine external commitments~Compare the consequences of delay rather than urgency wording alone~Distinguish importance from ease or emotional salience~Choose a feasible sequence and state what is deferred~Prepare renegotiation language for affected commitments when needed | every task is labeled urgent=>ask what happens if each waits=>consequences create a usable distinction~deferral affects another person=>plan explicit communication=>private reprioritization does not change their expectation | Task;Delay consequence;Priority choice;Follow-up | Fictional user Kian has a promised review and an optional workspace redesign in the same block. | Promised review;Another person waits;Prioritize or renegotiate;Confirm delivery expectation~Workspace redesign;No external dependency;Defer;Revisit after commitment | doing the easiest task first while hiding a missed promise | Fictional user Bea asks you to prioritize by title alone without knowing any consequences.')
add('GPR','focus-session-design | Focus session design | Specific work task;Known interruptions;Available session length | Choose a bounded output for the session~Prepare the inputs needed before starting~Identify likely interruptions and decide how to capture or handle them~Define a stopping condition tied to the output or available time~Review what actually interrupted progress before changing the next session | the task requires unavailable information=>resolve that dependency first=>a focus block cannot substitute for missing inputs~the session goal is broad=>reduce it to a visible artifact=>completion should be observable | Session element;Plan;Completion evidence;Adjustment | Fictional user Uma wants to draft a proposal but keeps searching for source notes mid-session. | Preparation;Collect source notes first;Notes accessible;Delay drafting until ready~Output;Draft problem paragraph;Paragraph exists;Review unresolved facts afterward | measuring a session only by sitting at a desk | Fictional user Davi wants a focus plan for work whose required documents are unavailable.')
add('GPR','personal-waiting-for-tracker | Waiting-for tracker | Delegated or requested items;Responsible contacts;Follow-up expectations | List requests that depend on another person or event~Record what was actually requested and when~Separate agreed response expectations from private hopes~Define a follow-up trigger for each item~Close the item only when the requested result is received or explicitly canceled | no response date was agreed=>label the follow-up as a personal reminder=>it is not a promise made by the other person~a reply does not answer the request=>keep the unresolved portion open=>any response is not necessarily completion | Waiting item;Request evidence;Expectation;Follow-up | Fictional user Lina requested a draft from a colleague who acknowledged receipt but gave no delivery date. | Draft request;Message retained;No agreed date;Ask about feasible timing~Acknowledgment;Receipt confirmed;Work not delivered;Keep request open | marking a dependency complete because someone replied thanks | Fictional user Tariq wants to escalate a request based on a deadline never communicated.')
add('GPR','personal-weekly-review | Weekly review agenda | Current tasks and projects;Recent commitments;Next-period constraints | Reconcile completed work with the task list~Check waiting items and stale commitments~Review each active project for a current next action~Compare upcoming obligations with realistic capacity~Choose changes to priorities and capture reasons | a project has no next action=>either define one or pause it explicitly=>an active label alone does not create progress~a task repeatedly rolls over=>inspect its relevance or obstacle=>copying it forward hides the issue | Review area;Observation;Decision;Next action | Fictional user Mira repeatedly carries a vague research task into each week. | Repeated carryover;Research remains vague;Clarify scope;Write one answerable question~Upcoming obligation;Known appointment;Reduce available work block;Adjust plan | copying last week list without checking why items remain | Fictional user Oren wants a weekly review that only celebrates completed tasks and ignores stalled commitments.')
add('GPR','routine-experiment-plan | Routine experiment plan | Desired behavior;Current obstacles;Feasible trial conditions | Define the behavior as an observable action~Identify the existing cue or context where it could occur~Reduce setup friction without changing the intended behavior~Choose a simple observation record and a review point~Adapt the routine based on what happened rather than a universal rule | the behavior depends on an unstable cue=>choose a more reliable context=>the routine needs an actual opportunity~misses have different causes=>record those differences=>one remedy may not fit every failure | Routine element;Proposed design;Observation;Adjustment | Fictional user Nia wants to review notes after meetings but often starts the next meeting immediately. | Cue;End of meeting;No gap available;Use next available transition~Action;Write unresolved decisions;Note created or not;Reduce scope when needed | promising a habit will form after a fixed universal number of days | Fictional user Eli wants a routine guaranteed to work despite unpredictable shifts.')
add('GPR','personal-information-retrieval-design | Personal information retrieval design | Frequently needed information;Current storage locations;Search habits | Identify the questions the person repeatedly needs to answer~Choose a stable naming or indexing convention tied to those questions~Separate reference material from active tasks~Test retrieval using realistic examples~Remove redundant categories that do not help find information | categories overlap heavily=>use clearer labels or cross-references=>uncertain filing slows retrieval~a document has several uses=>retain one source with links=>multiple uncontrolled copies can diverge | Information type;Retrieval question;Organization choice;Test | Fictional user Sora stores meeting decisions in several folders and cannot find the latest one. | Decision note;What was agreed?;Index by project and meeting reference;Find current decision~Source file;Where is original?;Keep one canonical location;Link from index | designing an elaborate folder tree before testing retrieval | Fictional user Max wants a new category for every possible future topic.')
add('GPR','overdue-commitment-recovery-plan | Overdue commitment recovery plan | Missed commitments;Affected people;Remaining work | Identify what was promised and what remains incomplete~Assess immediate consequences using known dependencies~Choose a realistic revised scope or timing~Draft an acknowledgment that states the miss and proposed repair~Track the repair without making another unsupported promise | the new completion estimate is uncertain=>offer a checkpoint rather than false certainty=>another missed promise compounds confusion~the commitment is no longer useful=>ask whether to cancel or replace it=>completion for its own sake may waste effort | Commitment;Current state;Repair proposal;Communication | Fictional user Ada missed a draft handoff and can provide an outline now but cannot confirm full completion. | Draft handoff;Incomplete;Offer outline and timing checkpoint;I missed the agreed handoff.~Next expectation;Full timing uncertain;Confirm after reviewing remaining work;I can share an outline now and update you after that review. | inventing a confident replacement deadline to reduce discomfort | Fictional user Ren asks for an apology that conceals the missed commitment.')

def build():
    rows=[]
    for d in DOMAINS:
        selected=[r for r in RECORDS if r['code']==d['code']]
        assert len(selected)==10, (d['code'],len(selected))
        for number,r in enumerate(selected,1):
            text,row=render(r,d,number)
            out=ROOT/'skills'/d['code']/r['slug']/'SKILL.md'
            out.parent.mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True)
            out.write_text(text,encoding='utf-8',newline='\n')
            rows.append(row)
    assert len(rows)==150
    manifest=dict(domains=[{k:v for k,v in d.items() if k not in ('context','risk')} for d in DOMAINS],skills=rows)
    target=ROOT/'staging'/'government_learning.json'
    target.parent.mkdir(exist_ok=True)
    target.write_text(json.dumps(manifest,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n',encoding='utf-8')


if __name__ == '__main__':
    import government_additions
    import government_review_repairs
    from government_artifacts_core import ARTIFACTS as CORE_ARTIFACTS
    from government_artifacts_education import ARTIFACTS as EDUCATION_ARTIFACTS
    government_additions.register(domain, add)
    government_review_repairs.apply(RECORDS)
    ARTIFACTS.update(CORE_ARTIFACTS)
    ARTIFACTS.update(EDUCATION_ARTIFACTS)
    ARTIFACTS.update(getattr(government_additions, 'ARTIFACTS', {}))
    build()

